#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056019 :tutorial/content "As we already know, to evaluate numerical expressions with a mix of different operations, we must follow the order of operations (PEMDAS).\n\nThe same rules apply to numerical expressions with negative numbers. For example, let's compute the value of\n\n$$\n(-2) \\times (-3)^2.\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.  First, we compute the exponents:\n\n$$\n(-2)\\,\\times\\overbrace{(-3)^2}^{\\large\\color{blue} 9}  = (-2) \\times {\\color{blue} 9}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we multiply:\n\n$$\n(-2) \\times 9 = -18\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(-2) \\times (-3)^2 = -18.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "70914b55-4296-53ed-8b9b-443bffeea358" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8083 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
